// Each entry below is an independently edited lesson: situation, method, example, and
// exercise are deliberately specific so that pages do not merely swap keywords.
export const guideDetails = {
  'active-listening-at-work': ['Active Listening at Work','communication','heard','Active listening at work is less about looking attentive and more about making the other person’s meaning usable. It prevents a task, concern, or decision from being lost behind familiar words.','Use the three-part loop: invite, reflect, confirm. Ask one open question, summarize the operational point in your own words, then ask whether you have it right.','A colleague says, “The handoff keeps breaking.” Try: “You’re seeing failures after the handoff, especially when ownership is unclear. Which handoff fails first?”','Do not turn a summary into a defense of your own work. Your first job is accuracy, not rebuttal.','In your next one-to-one, write down one exact phrase the other person uses. Reflect its meaning before offering your own solution.'],
  'prepare-for-a-difficult-conversation': ['How to Prepare for a Difficult Conversation','communication','heard','Difficult conversations improve when preparation narrows the issue instead of rehearsing every grievance. Decide what needs to change and what evidence makes that request fair.','Make a one-page brief: facts you observed, effect on work or trust, your request, and a question that gives the other person room to explain.','Instead of “You never keep me informed,” say, “The launch date changed twice without an update. I need a note the same day a deadline moves. What would make that workable?”','Avoid diagnosing motives. You can describe a late update; you cannot reliably prove why it happened.','Practice the first two sentences aloud. If they contain “always,” “never,” or a label, rewrite them as observable facts.'],
  'give-feedback-that-helps': ['How to Give Feedback That Helps','communication','heard','Useful feedback identifies a behavior, its consequence, and a next attempt. It gives someone a handle they can actually use, rather than a verdict on their talent.','Use the BEC sequence: behavior, effect, change. Name a moment, explain what it caused, and make one concrete request for the next version.','“In the client call, the recommendation came after six minutes of background. The client had trouble locating the decision. Next time, lead with the recommendation and put context after it.”','Do not bury the request under praise. Warmth matters, but the recipient should still know what to repeat or change.','Choose one recent piece of work and draft feedback in three sentences: behavior, effect, change.'],
  'receive-feedback-without-getting-defensive': ['How to Receive Feedback Without Getting Defensive','communication','heard','Feedback can feel like a threat because it compresses a judgment into a short moment. A pause lets you collect evidence before you decide whether the feedback is accurate or useful.','Separate listening from agreeing. First ask for an example, then summarize it, then decide what experiment would test the suggestion.','Say, “Can you show me where that became confusing?” rather than “That was intentional.” You may later keep the choice, but you will have better information.','Do not promise a change before you understand the problem. A precise follow-up is more credible than instant agreement.','Next time feedback surprises you, ask for one example and wait until the next day before choosing a response.'],
  'set-assertive-boundaries': ['How to Set Assertive Boundaries','communication','heard','An assertive boundary tells people what you can do, what you cannot do, and what alternative keeps the work moving. It is clearer than a vague apology or an angry refusal.','Name the limit, give a brief reason when useful, and offer a bounded option. Keep the explanation shorter than the request.','“I can review this by Thursday, not today. If the decision is needed sooner, I can review the two highest-risk sections this afternoon.”','Do not over-explain. Long justifications invite a negotiation over your personal circumstances instead of the work.','Write one boundary for a recurring request using the pattern: I can ___; I cannot ___; I can offer ___.'],
  'run-a-better-one-to-one': ['How to Run a Better One-to-One','professional conversations','heard','A useful one-to-one is a protected space for priorities, obstacles, and development—not a status meeting with a friendlier name.','Use a repeatable agenda: wins, decisions needed, blockers, and one longer-term question. Send it early enough for both people to add items.','Ask, “What is taking more energy than it should?” This often reveals a system problem that a task list will miss.','Do not fill the meeting with updates that belong in a document. Save the conversation for ambiguity, tradeoffs, and support.','Bring one blocker and one question about the next month to your next one-to-one.'],
  'write-clearer-work-emails': ['How to Write Clearer Work Emails','professional conversations','heard','A clear work email lets a busy reader identify the point, the action, and the deadline in seconds. Good writing is a service to the decision-maker.','Put the requested action in the first two lines. Use short headings for context, options, and deadline; move background material below the request.','Subject: “Approve vendor option B by Wednesday.” Opening: “Please approve option B by 3 p.m. Wednesday so procurement can place the order.”','Do not make the reader infer the question from a long narrative. If there is no decision or action, consider whether email is needed.','Before sending one email today, underline the sentence that asks for action. Move it to the top if it is not already there.'],
  'handle-meeting-disagreements': ['How to Handle Meeting Disagreements','professional conversations','heard','Meeting disagreement becomes productive when people compare assumptions and consequences rather than compete for airtime.','State the shared decision, name the assumption you see differently, and propose a way to test it. This keeps the room oriented toward evidence.','“We both want the rollout to be stable. I’m less confident that two weeks is enough for testing. Can we list the failure modes and estimate each one?”','Do not repeat a point more loudly when it is not landing. Ask what evidence the group is using and join that frame.','In your next disagreement, begin by naming the shared goal before stating your concern.'],
  'introduce-yourself-with-confidence': ['How to Introduce Yourself with Confidence','public speaking','heard','A confident introduction gives people a simple way to remember your work and why it matters. It does not need a biography or a performance voice.','Use three parts: role, audience or problem, and current focus. Add one detail that gives the listener a useful follow-up question.','“I help product teams turn customer interviews into clearer decisions. Right now I’m working on a faster way to spot repeated objections.”','Do not list every credential. Choose the detail that makes the present conversation easier to start.','Write a 25-word introduction and say it to a friend without memorizing every word.'],
  'structure-a-short-presentation': ['How to Structure a Short Presentation','public speaking','heard','Short presentations need a visible route. The audience should know what you want them to understand or decide before they receive the supporting material.','Use point, proof, proposal. State the main conclusion, offer two pieces of evidence, then make the specific ask or recommendation.','For a five-minute update: “We should delay launch one week. Two critical tests are failing. I recommend moving the date and using Monday for retesting.”','Do not spend most of a short talk on chronology. Background belongs only when it changes the decision.','Turn your next update into three headings: conclusion, evidence, request.'],
  'manage-presentation-nerves': ['How to Manage Presentation Nerves','public speaking','heard','Nerves usually signal that the situation matters. The goal is not to erase them; it is to give attention a useful job before and during the talk.','Prepare the opening, the first transition, and the closing request. Then rehearse under slightly realistic conditions: standing up, timing yourself, and recovering after a pause.','If your heart races before speaking, place both feet on the floor and silently name the first sentence—not the whole presentation.','Do not respond to anxiety by adding more slides. Extra material gives you more decisions to manage while speaking.','Record a two-minute version of your talk. Note one place you rushed and repeat only that transition.'],
  'answer-questions-after-a-talk': ['How to Answer Questions After a Talk','public speaking','heard','A good answer begins by making sure you heard the question. That small pause protects you from answering the version you expected instead of the one asked.','Use clarify, answer, connect. Clarify the question if needed, give the direct answer first, then connect it to the main decision or evidence.','“If I understand you, you’re asking whether this applies to small teams too. Yes—the smallest version is a weekly review, and the same principle is to compare results before changing the process.”','Do not use questions as a stage for a second speech. Answer the person, then offer to continue afterward if the topic is broader.','Ask a friend to pose three skeptical questions about your next talk. Practice answering each in under 45 seconds.'],
  'practice-a-skill-in-small-parts': ['How to Practice a Skill in Small Parts','skill acquisition','learning','Many skills feel overwhelming because several subskills happen at once. Breaking practice into parts lets you improve the weakest component without confusing it with the whole performance.','Name the real task, list the component moves, then isolate one move for a short drill before recombining it with the full task.','For cooking, you might practice knife consistency for ten minutes, then return to a full recipe. For speaking, practice only the opening before rehearsing the full talk.','Do not stay in drills forever. Recombine the part with the real task so you learn when to use it.','Choose one skill you are learning. List five component moves and pick the one that currently creates the most errors.'],
  'choose-the-right-practice-difficulty': ['How to Choose the Right Practice Difficulty','deliberate practice','learning','Practice should be hard enough to reveal errors and manageable enough to correct them. The useful zone is often uncomfortable but not chaotic.','Adjust one difficulty lever at a time: speed, complexity, support, or consequences. Keep a version you can complete with care.','If you can play a passage perfectly at half speed but not full speed, increase tempo in small steps instead of repeating full-speed mistakes.','Do not mistake exhaustion for difficulty. If attention is gone, lower volume or stop; errors made without reflection teach little.','Rate your last practice session from 1 to 5 for challenge. If it was a 1 or 5, change one lever for the next session.'],
  'use-a-practice-log': ['How to Use a Practice Log','practice systems','learning','A practice log turns vague impressions into a small record of what you tried, what happened, and what to do next. It is not a diary of hours.','After each session, record the target, the drill or task, one observation, and the next adjustment. Keep it short enough to maintain.','“Target: clearer transitions. Tried: three two-minute explanations. Observation: I lost the thread after the example. Next: write one bridge sentence first.”','Do not log every minute. The value is in decisions that make the next attempt more intelligent.','Create a four-line note for your next session: target, task, observation, next adjustment.'],
  'build-a-weekly-review': ['How to Build a Weekly Review for Learning','learning methods','learning','A weekly review gives learning projects a steering mechanism. It helps you notice whether effort is producing the capability you actually wanted.','Set aside 20 minutes to review evidence, identify one bottleneck, choose the next week’s sessions, and remove one friction point.','Compare a baseline recording from Monday with a Friday attempt. Instead of asking “Did I work hard?”, ask “What can I do now that I could not do then?”','Do not redesign the entire system every week. Change the smallest thing that addresses the clearest bottleneck.','Schedule a 20-minute review and bring one artifact from this week’s work.'],
  'learn-from-examples': ['How to Learn from Examples Without Copying','study strategies','learning','Examples can reveal structure that explanations alone hide. The goal is to notice decisions, then make your own choices in a comparable situation.','Study an example twice: first for the overall shape, then with annotations explaining why each step serves the goal. Close it before you attempt your own version.','When studying an effective email, label the subject line, request, evidence, deadline, and close. Then write an email about a different decision from scratch.','Do not imitate surface wording. Copying phrases can mask the fact that you have not understood the underlying choice.','Take one strong example in your field and annotate three decisions it makes before producing a new version.'],
  'remember-what-you-study': ['How to Remember What You Study','study strategies','learning','Memory improves when you practice retrieving and using ideas, not when you only reread them. Retrieval reveals what is actually available without the page.','After a short study block, close the material and write what you can recall: main claim, example, and one question. Check the source only after attempting recall.','After reading about a concept, explain it from memory to an imaginary colleague in five sentences. Mark the point where you needed to look back.','Do not treat a familiar-looking page as proof of learning. Recognition is easier than recall.','At the end of your next study session, do a two-minute closed-book summary.'],
  'plan-a-learning-project': ['How to Plan a Learning Project','learning methods','learning','A learning project needs a concrete finish line, evidence of progress, and a rhythm that fits ordinary life. “Get better” is too broad to guide a week of work.','Define a performance task, a date, a baseline, and a small weekly cadence. Then identify the smallest subskill most likely to improve the task.','Instead of “learn photography,” choose “make ten well-exposed indoor portraits by June,” then practice light measurement and review each photo.','Do not build a complicated curriculum before you have tried the first task. Early attempts reveal what you actually need.','Write a one-sentence project outcome and the artifact that will show whether you reached it.'],
  'avoid-perfectionism-when-learning': ['How to Avoid Perfectionism When Learning','long-term improvement','learning','Perfectionism often protects you from seeing an imperfect attempt. Improvement requires visible attempts because they create the information that planning cannot provide.','Set an output quota and a quality review separately. First make the small attempt; later choose one criterion to improve on the next version.','A writer can draft 300 rough words, then review only whether the opening makes a clear claim. That is more useful than waiting for a flawless first sentence.','Do not call avoidance “high standards.” Standards help only when they lead to a specific next action.','Choose a tiny finished artifact for today: one explanation, one sketch, one drill, or one recording.'],
  'recover-after-a-bad-practice-session': ['How to Recover After a Bad Practice Session','long-term improvement','learning','A bad session can be data rather than a verdict. The useful question is what condition, target, or method made the work fall apart.','Run a short reset: describe the failure without labels, identify one variable, and plan an easier re-entry session. Preserve continuity rather than trying to compensate with intensity.','“I missed the same transition five times after 40 minutes” suggests reducing session length or isolating the transition—not doubling tomorrow’s practice.','Do not erase the log or abandon the project after one poor day. A record makes patterns visible.','Write one neutral sentence about your last poor session and one smaller task for the next attempt.'],
  'measure-progress-with-a-baseline': ['How to Measure Progress with a Baseline','long-term improvement','learning','A baseline gives later work something honest to compare against. It makes improvement visible even when daily effort feels uneven.','Choose one representative task, record the conditions, and keep the first attempt. Repeat under similar conditions after several practice sessions.','For speaking, record a two-minute explanation of the same topic today and again in two weeks. Compare clarity, pauses, and whether the listener can state the main point.','Do not change the test every time. A moving target can make progress impossible to see.','Create one baseline attempt this week and write down the date, task, and conditions.'],
  'ask-for-useful-help': ['How to Ask for Useful Help','communication','heard','People can help more effectively when they know the context, the decision, and the kind of help you want. A focused request respects their time and improves the answer.','Give a 30-second context, name the obstacle, and ask for one type of response: an example, a critique, a decision, or a resource.','“I’m preparing a five-minute update for a skeptical audience. Could you tell me where the recommendation becomes unclear?” is easier to answer than “Can you help?”','Do not send a large document with no question. Make the first action for the helper obvious.','Before your next request, add one sentence that states exactly what a helpful response would contain.'],
  'lead-a-decision-making-conversation': ['How to Lead a Decision-Making Conversation','professional conversations','heard','Decision conversations stall when the group is discussing an issue without agreeing on what choice is actually required. Facilitation begins by making the decision visible.','Open with the decision, the deadline, and the criteria. Near the end, summarize options, owner, and next action aloud.','“By the end of this meeting we need to choose a launch date. The criteria are testing readiness, customer impact, and staffing. I’ll summarize the choice before we leave.”','Do not let a list of opinions substitute for a decision. Ask what evidence or authority will resolve the open point.','Use the first minute of your next meeting to state the decision and criteria in one sentence.'],
  'speak-up-when-you-disagree': ['How to Speak Up When You Disagree','assertiveness','heard','Speaking up is easier when you treat disagreement as a contribution to a shared goal. You do not need absolute certainty to name a risk or ask a better question.','Use a soft start and a precise concern: acknowledge the goal, name the assumption, and invite examination of the risk.','“I support moving quickly. I’m concerned we are assuming the migration can be reversed easily. What is our rollback plan if the first release fails?”','Do not wait until you have a complete alternative. A well-formed question can be the right first contribution.','Write one sentence you could use to name a concern without claiming certainty.'],
  'make-time-for-consistent-practice': ['How to Make Time for Consistent Practice','practice systems','learning','Consistency usually comes from lowering the activation energy of a session, not from finding heroic motivation. Make the next action obvious and small.','Choose a reliable cue, prepare the materials in advance, and define a minimum version that still counts on crowded days.','Keep a guitar on a stand and define “five focused minutes on one transition” as the minimum session. Longer practice can follow, but it is not required.','Do not use a missed day as evidence that the routine failed. Resume with the minimum version at the next cue.','Set up one visible cue for your practice and define a five-minute minimum session.'],
  'turn-mistakes-into-feedback': ['How to Turn Mistakes into Feedback','feedback','learning','A mistake becomes feedback only when you can identify what happened, compare it with a standard, and choose a different attempt. Simply noticing an error is not enough.','Use the loop: capture, classify, correct. Save the attempt, label the type of error, then design one correction that can be tested immediately.','If a listener cannot summarize your explanation, classify the error as structure rather than “bad speaking,” then test a one-sentence point-first opening.','Do not collect mistakes without changing the next attempt. A log should lead to a decision.','Review one recent error and write its category plus the smallest correction you can test.'],
  'practice-explaining-complex-ideas': ['How to Practice Explaining Complex Ideas','communication','heard','Complex ideas become clearer when you decide what the listener needs to understand first. Explanation is a sequence of choices about relevance, not a dump of everything you know.','Start with the practical question, give a plain-language answer, add one example, and only then introduce necessary terminology.','To explain compound interest, begin with “Your savings earn returns on earlier returns,” then show a simple $100 example before discussing formulas.','Do not begin with every exception. Give the core model first and add nuance when the listener needs it.','Explain one complex idea to a non-specialist in three sentences, then ask them to repeat the main point.'],
  'build-confidence-through-evidence': ['How to Build Confidence Through Evidence','long-term improvement','learning','Durable confidence is the ability to point to preparation, repetitions, and recovery from mistakes. It grows from evidence rather than constant self-reassurance.','Keep a small evidence file: completed attempts, feedback received, problems solved, and skills you can now perform under normal conditions. Review it before demanding situations.','Before a presentation, read three notes from prior talks: a successful opening, one question you handled, and a correction you learned to make.','Do not use the evidence file to deny difficulty. Use it to remember that you can prepare and adapt.','Add one item to an evidence file after your next meaningful attempt.']
};

Object.assign(guideDetails, {
  'how-to-state-a-clear-recommendation': ['How to State a Clear Recommendation','decision communication','heard','A recommendation is useful when a listener can tell what you want done, why now, and what tradeoff you accept.','Lead with the choice in one sentence. Follow with the two strongest reasons and name the main risk so nobody has to guess what you considered.','“Choose vendor B this week. It meets the deadline and has the lower integration risk; its support hours are the tradeoff.”','Do not begin with every fact you collected. That makes the listener assemble the recommendation for you.','Turn a current decision into one choice, two reasons, and one acknowledged tradeoff.'],
  'how-to-summarize-a-meeting': ['How to Summarize a Meeting Clearly','meeting communication','heard','A meeting summary protects decisions from fading into different memories once people leave the room.','Write the decision, owner, deadline, and unresolved question in that order. Send it while the context is still fresh.','“Decision: test option A. Owner: Priya. First result due Friday. Open question: whether legal review is required.”','Do not write a chronological transcript. Readers need commitments, not a replay.','After your next meeting, send a four-line summary before starting the next task.'],
  'how-to-ask-for-a-decision': ['How to Ask for a Decision','decision communication','heard','Decision requests work when the reader knows the exact choice and the cost of waiting.','Name the decision, deadline, options, and your recommendation. Put supporting detail below those four facts.','“Please choose the launch date by Tuesday: May 4 or May 11. I recommend May 11 because testing needs four more days.”','Do not end with a vague “thoughts?” when approval is actually required.','Rewrite one pending message so its first sentence states the needed decision and date.'],
  'how-to-clarify-ambiguous-requests': ['How to Clarify an Ambiguous Request','work communication','heard','Ambiguous requests create rework because people fill gaps with different assumptions.','Reflect what you think the outcome is, then ask about scope, audience, and deadline. Offer a small default only when it helps move forward.','“I can prepare the client brief. Is the goal an internal decision or a customer-facing document, and when do you need it?”','Do not silently choose the broadest interpretation. It often creates unnecessary work.','Use three questions—outcome, audience, deadline—on the next request that feels underspecified.'],
  'how-to-give-a-concise-update': ['How to Give a Concise Project Update','work communication','heard','A concise update lets people assess status without searching through activity.','Use status, evidence, risk, and next step. Limit each part to the information that changes a decision.','“On track: prototype is approved. Risk: supplier quote is late. Next: confirm cost by Thursday.”','Do not confuse a list of tasks with a useful status report.','Draft a four-sentence update for one active project and remove any detail that changes nothing.'],
  'how-to-listen-for-the-real-question': ['How to Listen for the Real Question','listening','heard','The first question someone asks is sometimes a doorway to a concern they have not yet named.','Answer briefly, then ask what decision or worry sits behind it. Listen for stakes, not only wording.','When asked “Can this wait?”, try “It can. Are you weighing this against another deadline?”','Do not treat every question as a request for a lecture.','In a conversation this week, ask one follow-up about why the answer matters.'],
  'how-to-use-pauses-when-speaking': ['How to Use Pauses When Speaking','public speaking','heard','A pause gives listeners time to process and gives you time to choose the next sentence.','Pause after your central point, before an important number, and when a question surprises you. Breathe rather than filling the space.','Say, “The recommendation is to delay.” Pause. Then explain the evidence.','Do not apologize for a short silence. Most listeners experience it as emphasis, not failure.','Mark three pause points in the opening of your next talk and rehearse them aloud.'],
  'how-to-make-a-request-without-apologizing': ['How to Make a Request Without Over-Apologizing','assertiveness','heard','A clear request respects the other person’s ability to say yes, no, or negotiate.','State what you need, why it matters, and a reasonable deadline. Use “please” without turning the request into a self-criticism.','“Please send the draft by Wednesday so I can include it in the review.”','Do not open with several apologies that obscure the actual request.','Replace one “Sorry to bother you” in a draft message with the request itself.'],
  'how-to-handle-an-unexpected-question': ['How to Handle an Unexpected Question','public speaking','heard','Unexpected questions are easier when you separate understanding the question from producing an answer.','Pause, restate the question, give the most direct answer you can, and say what you will check if evidence is missing.','“You are asking about cost after year one. I do not have the final figure, but I can confirm it tomorrow.”','Do not invent certainty to avoid a pause.','Practice answering one hard question with: clarify, answer, follow up.'],
  'how-to-de-escalate-a-tense-conversation': ['How to De-escalate a Tense Conversation','conflict communication','heard','Tension drops when both people can see the issue without having to defend their identity.','Slow the pace, name the shared goal, and return to observable facts. Ask one question that makes room for the other account.','“We both want the handoff to work. Can we look at what happened Tuesday before deciding why?”','Do not demand agreement while emotions are rising.','Prepare one neutral sentence that names a shared goal for a recurring conflict.'],
  'how-to-say-no-to-extra-work': ['How to Say No to Extra Work','boundaries','heard','Saying no well protects existing commitments while showing what is still possible.','Name your current constraint, decline the added scope, and offer one bounded alternative if you genuinely can.','“I cannot take the full review this week. I can comment on the two highest-risk sections by Friday.”','Do not promise an impossible timeline to avoid discomfort.','Choose one request and write a limit plus one realistic option.'],
  'how-to-follow-up-after-no-response': ['How to Follow Up After No Response','work communication','heard','A good follow-up makes it easy to respond without sounding accusatory.','Restate the decision or question, name the deadline, and offer a simple next action.','“Following up on the budget choice below. Could you confirm A or B by Thursday so we can book the vendor?”','Do not resend a long message with no indication of what changed.','Write a follow-up whose first line contains the decision and due date.'],
  'how-to-prepare-for-a-performance-review': ['How to Prepare for a Performance Review','professional development','heard','Preparation turns a performance review into a conversation about evidence and next steps.','Gather outcomes, feedback, obstacles, and goals. Pair each accomplishment with the context and impact that make it understandable.','Instead of “I led the launch,” say “I coordinated the launch plan, which let the team ship two weeks earlier.”','Do not rely on memory in the meeting. Bring a short evidence list.','Create four headings—results, feedback, obstacles, goals—and add two facts under each.'],
  'how-to-ask-for-feedback-on-a-draft': ['How to Ask for Feedback on a Draft','feedback','heard','Specific feedback requests produce better comments and reduce the burden on the reviewer.','Name the audience, decision, and one or two questions you want tested. Give the reviewer a due date and the version to comment on.','“Could you tell me whether the recommendation is clear to a finance reader and where the evidence feels thin?”','Do not ask “Any feedback?” on a large document.','Send one draft with two targeted review questions.'],
  'how-to-disagree-with-your-manager': ['How to Disagree with Your Manager Respectfully','workplace communication','heard','Disagreeing upward is most useful when it helps your manager make a better decision, not when it proves independence.','Begin with the shared outcome, state your evidence, and ask how they are weighing the tradeoff.','“I support the deadline. I am concerned the current plan leaves no testing buffer. How are you weighing that risk?”','Do not frame the discussion as a contest you must win.','Prepare one evidence-based concern and one question before your next disagreement.'],
  'how-to-give-credit-at-work': ['How to Give Credit at Work','workplace communication','heard','Giving credit accurately builds trust and makes collaboration visible.','Name the person, the contribution, and its effect. Be specific enough that the recognition teaches people what helped.','“Mina simplified the onboarding flow, which cut the support questions we received this week.”','Do not use generic praise when a concrete contribution is available.','Thank one collaborator by naming what they did and why it mattered.'],
  'how-to-handle-criticism-in-public': ['How to Handle Criticism in Public','feedback','heard','Public criticism can trigger a fast defensive response. A short, calm reply protects the work and gives you time to assess the point.','Acknowledge the concern, ask for a concrete example, and move detailed debate to a better setting when needed.','“I hear the concern about the timeline. Which assumption looks least realistic to you?”','Do not counterattack to regain status.','Practice a one-sentence response that asks for an example rather than arguing.'],
  'how-to-end-a-conversation-politely': ['How to End a Conversation Politely','conversation skills','heard','Ending a conversation clearly is kinder than lingering while your attention has already moved elsewhere.','Signal appreciation, state the transition, and name any next step.','“This was useful. I need to join another call now; I will send the notes this afternoon.”','Do not disappear mid-conversation when a simple closing will do.','Use a clear closing sentence once this week instead of waiting for an awkward exit.'],
  'how-to-build-rapport-without-small-talk': ['How to Build Rapport Without Forced Small Talk','conversation skills','heard','Rapport grows from attention and relevant curiosity; it does not require a performance of charm.','Ask about the person’s current work, choice, or perspective, then share a related detail without taking over.','“What made that project the priority this quarter?” invites a more useful answer than a generic opener.','Do not interrogate someone with question after question.','Prepare two genuine questions about the setting you expect to be in.'],
  'how-to-prioritize-when-everything-feels-urgent': ['How to Prioritize When Everything Feels Urgent','decision making','learning','When everything looks urgent, compare consequences rather than reacting to the loudest request.','List deadlines, impact, dependencies, and reversibility. Choose the task whose delay causes the most difficult-to-recover harm.','A request due today may wait if a decision due tomorrow blocks five other people.','Do not treat other people’s anxiety as the only priority signal.','Sort your current tasks by consequence of delay and identify the first move.'],
  'how-to-break-down-a-complex-skill': ['How to Break Down a Complex Skill','skill acquisition','learning','A complex skill becomes learnable when you identify the moves that produce the final performance.','Start with one real task, observe its stages, and label knowledge, physical actions, and judgment separately.','For interviewing, separate question design, listening, note-taking, follow-ups, and synthesis.','Do not make a list so broad that nothing can be practiced.','Choose one task and list its component moves before selecting a single drill.'],
  'how-to-set-a-learning-goal': ['How to Set a Learning Goal You Can Test','learning methods','learning','A useful learning goal describes what you will be able to do, not merely what you will study.','Name a performance, conditions, quality standard, and date. This gives practice a target and makes review possible.','“By June 1, explain our product in two minutes to a new customer who can state the main benefit.”','Do not use goals such as “understand more” without evidence of performance.','Rewrite one learning ambition as a task, standard, and date.'],
  'how-to-choose-a-practice-task': ['How to Choose a Practice Task','practice design','learning','The right practice task resembles the real work while staying small enough to repeat and review.','Choose a task that exposes the subskill you need, can be completed often, and has a clear standard.','A two-minute explanation is a better speaking drill than reading more advice about speaking.','Do not choose a task only because it feels comfortable.','Select one repeatable task you can complete three times this week.'],
  'how-to-practice-under-pressure': ['How to Practice Under Pressure','performance practice','learning','Pressure practice helps you preserve a skill when time, attention, or stakes change.','Master a calmer version first, then add one constraint: time limit, audience, distraction, or consequence.','After rehearsing a talk alone, deliver it to a friend with a timer and two questions at the end.','Do not add every stressor at once.','Choose one mastered drill and add one small realistic constraint.'],
  'how-to-learn-from-a-mistake-at-work': ['How to Learn from a Mistake at Work','reflection','learning','A work mistake becomes useful when you can improve the system or decision that allowed it.','Describe what happened, separate contributing conditions from blame, and identify one safeguard for next time.','“The wrong file was sent because the final review step had no owner; assign one owner before external sends.”','Do not turn a retrospective into a search for a single guilty person.','Review one error by writing event, conditions, and a preventive change.'],
  'how-to-build-a-study-plan': ['How to Build a Study Plan','study strategies','learning','A study plan works when it allocates attention to retrieval, practice, and review—not only reading.','Start from the performance you need, schedule short study blocks, and attach each block to a visible output or self-test.','For an exam, alternate a concept review with closed-book questions and an error review.','Do not fill every hour before discovering what you miss.','Plan three study sessions, each with one output you can check.'],
  'how-to-use-retrieval-practice': ['How to Use Retrieval Practice','study strategies','learning','Retrieval practice strengthens access to knowledge by making you recall it before looking at the source.','Close the material, answer a question or explain the idea, then compare your attempt with the source and correct gaps.','After a chapter, write the three main claims from memory before reopening the book.','Do not confuse highlighting with retrieval.','Make five questions from your next study section and answer them without notes.'],
  'how-to-review-after-a-practice-session': ['How to Review After a Practice Session','practice reflection','learning','A short review converts effort into information for the next session.','Record the target, one result, one error pattern, and the single adjustment you will test next.','“Target: cleaner transitions. Result: opening improved. Error: rushed after the example. Next: pause before the bridge.”','Do not write a diary instead of a decision.','End your next session with four lines: target, result, pattern, next adjustment.'],
  'how-to-keep-going-after-slow-progress': ['How to Keep Going After Slow Progress','motivation','learning','Slow progress is easier to tolerate when you can see evidence that the process is still producing information.','Return to a baseline, narrow the target, and compare weekly attempts rather than judging each day.','A musician may not hear daily improvement but can compare recordings made two weeks apart.','Do not increase volume blindly when the bottleneck is unclear.','Compare one current attempt with an older baseline and choose one next adjustment.']
});

Object.assign(guideDetails, {
  'how-to-write-a-strong-opening': ['How to Write a Strong Opening','writing','heard','An opening earns attention by giving readers a reason to continue and a clear sense of where the piece is going.','Name the problem, point, or decision early. Add only the context that helps a reader understand why it matters now.','“We should simplify the approval process because the current version delays routine work by three days.”','Do not warm up with history that the reader does not yet need.','Rewrite the first two sentences of a draft so they state the point and its relevance.'],
  'how-to-write-a-clear-subject-line': ['How to Write a Clear Email Subject Line','writing','heard','A subject line helps a busy reader decide what requires attention before opening the email.','Use a concrete noun and action or deadline when relevant. Match the line to the decision inside the message.','“Review Q3 budget options by Friday” is clearer than “Budget.”','Do not use vague labels that force people to open the message to understand its purpose.','Draft three subject lines for a current email and choose the one that reveals the action.'],
  'how-to-turn-notes-into-an-action-plan': ['How to Turn Notes into an Action Plan','planning','learning','Notes become useful when they lead to a choice about what will happen next.','Sort notes into decisions, actions, questions, and reference material. Give each action an owner and a date.','A note reading “customer wants faster setup” becomes “Test a shortened setup flow; Jordan owns a first draft by Tuesday.”','Do not keep a long list of observations without deciding which one changes behavior.','Review one page of notes and convert three items into owned actions or explicit questions.'],
  'how-to-make-a-decision-with-incomplete-information': ['How to Make a Decision with Incomplete Information','decision making','learning','Most practical decisions arrive before every fact is available. The goal is to know what uncertainty matters.','Define the decision, list the unknowns, and identify which unknown could reverse the choice. Seek only that evidence before acting.','If choosing a vendor, the unknown that changes the choice may be data handling, not a minor feature difference.','Do not postpone a reversible decision until certainty feels comfortable.','Name one decision, its key uncertainty, and the cheapest way to reduce it.'],
  'how-to-compare-two-options': ['How to Compare Two Options Clearly','decision making','learning','A comparison is useful when both options are measured against the same criteria.','Choose three to five criteria tied to the outcome, weight what matters most, and record the tradeoff you accept.','Compare two training programs on practice time, feedback quality, cost, and schedule—not on a vague “feel.”','Do not add criteria after you see which option you prefer.','Make a two-column comparison using four criteria for one pending choice.'],
  'how-to-avoid-decision-fatigue': ['How to Avoid Decision Fatigue','decision making','learning','Decision fatigue is reduced by reserving attention for choices that genuinely require judgment.','Standardize repeatable choices, prepare defaults, and group similar decisions into a short review window.','Keep a reusable meeting agenda so you do not redesign the structure before every conversation.','Do not automate choices that need fresh context or human care.','Identify one recurring low-stakes choice and create a sensible default.'],
  'how-to-learn-from-expert-feedback': ['How to Learn from Expert Feedback','feedback','learning','Expert feedback is most valuable when you turn it into an observable test rather than a verdict.','Ask for the specific moment, principle, and next attempt. Then test one change while keeping other conditions stable.','A coach saying “your timing is late” becomes “start the movement one beat earlier in the next three attempts.”','Do not collect expert opinions without practicing the correction.','Ask one reviewer for a single next attempt you can test this week.'],
  'how-to-spot-a-learning-bottleneck': ['How to Spot a Learning Bottleneck','skill acquisition','learning','A bottleneck is the part of a process that limits the quality of the whole performance.','Review errors for patterns, then isolate the earliest recurring breakdown. Improve that before optimizing what already works.','In writing, unclear claims may be the bottleneck even if grammar also needs work.','Do not fix the most visible symptom if it is caused by an earlier step.','Look at three recent attempts and name the earliest repeated difficulty.'],
  'how-to-create-a-practice-feedback-loop': ['How to Create a Practice Feedback Loop','practice design','learning','A feedback loop connects an attempt to evidence and then to a better next attempt.','Define the standard before practicing, capture the result, compare it, and select one adjustment.','Record a sales explanation, ask whether a listener can repeat the value, then revise only the opening.','Do not wait for vague feelings of improvement.','Build one loop with a task, a standard, and a source of evidence.'],
  'how-to-practice-a-presentation-alone': ['How to Practice a Presentation Alone','public speaking','heard','Solo rehearsal works when it simulates enough of the real task to reveal problems.','Rehearse standing, use a timer, record one run, and review the opening, transitions, and close separately.','A recording can show that a clear slide is paired with an unclear spoken transition.','Do not rehearse only in your head.','Record a two-minute section and note one change for the next run.'],
  'how-to-rehearse-a-difficult-conversation': ['How to Rehearse a Difficult Conversation','conflict communication','heard','Rehearsal helps you choose words and limits before emotion makes the situation harder.','Practice your opening, the evidence, and one response to pushback. Keep room for the other person’s real perspective.','“I want to discuss the missed handoff and agree on a process that prevents it” is a calmer opening than a list of grievances.','Do not script every line; conversation is not a performance you can control.','Say your first two sentences aloud and remove labels or absolute claims.'],
  'how-to-set-an-agenda-for-a-meeting': ['How to Set a Useful Meeting Agenda','meeting communication','heard','An agenda tells participants why they are meeting and what preparation will matter.','List the decision or outcome first, then topics, pre-reading, and time limits. Invite additions before the meeting starts.','“Decide the launch owner; review risks; assign next steps” is an agenda people can prepare for.','Do not use “updates” as the only agenda item when a decision is expected.','Write the outcome sentence for your next meeting before listing topics.'],
  'how-to-facilitate-a-quiet-meeting': ['How to Facilitate a Quiet Meeting','meeting facilitation','heard','A quiet meeting may reflect uncertainty, hierarchy, or a question that is too broad.','Give people a minute to write, invite specific perspectives, and ask for evidence rather than generic opinions.','“Take one minute: what is the biggest implementation risk?” gives a clearer entry point than “Any thoughts?”','Do not interpret silence as agreement.','Use a written prompt before the next question that needs group input.'],
  'how-to-handle-a-dominant-conversationalist': ['How to Handle a Dominant Conversationalist','meeting facilitation','heard','A dominant speaker can crowd out useful information without intending to.','Acknowledge the contribution, redirect to the question, and invite someone who has not spoken.','“That is one important view. I want to hear from Lee, who worked on the support side.”','Do not shame the person; change the structure of the conversation.','Prepare one redirecting sentence before a meeting with uneven participation.'],
  'how-to-ask-a-good-follow-up-question': ['How to Ask a Good Follow-Up Question','conversation skills','heard','Follow-up questions show that you are tracking meaning rather than waiting for your turn.','Pick up a concrete word, decision, or tension in the answer and ask what shaped it.','“You said the handoff felt risky. Which part created the risk?”','Do not pivot to your own story before understanding the answer.','In your next conversation, ask one question using the speaker’s own wording.'],
  'how-to-speak-more-slowly': ['How to Speak More Slowly Without Sounding-Unnatural','public speaking','heard','Speaking more slowly is easier when you create structure that gives you places to pause.','Shorten sentences, mark transitions, and let punctuation guide your breath. Focus on clarity rather than trying to mechanically reduce speed.','After “The risk is timing,” pause before explaining the cause.','Do not stretch every word; use meaningful pauses instead.','Record one minute of speech and add pauses after each main point.'],
  'how-to-explain-a-process': ['How to Explain a Process Clearly','communication','heard','Process explanations work when they show sequence, purpose, and the point where judgment is needed.','Start with the outcome, then give the steps in order and name any decision rule that changes the path.','“First verify the request, then route it; escalate only if the request affects an existing contract.”','Do not list steps without explaining what success looks like.','Explain a routine process to someone unfamiliar with it and ask where they would hesitate.'],
  'how-to-use-an-example-to-explain-an-idea': ['How to Use an Example to Explain an Idea','communication','heard','An example makes an abstract idea easier to test against a real situation.','Choose a familiar situation, keep it short, and explicitly connect the detail back to the principle.','To explain opportunity cost, compare choosing one evening project with giving up another use of that time.','Do not let the example become a second complicated topic.','Add one concrete example to an explanation you are preparing.'],
  'how-to-prepare-for-a-job-interview': ['How to Prepare for a Job Interview','professional development','heard','Interview preparation is strongest when it connects your evidence to the work the employer needs done.','Study the role, select stories with clear actions and outcomes, and prepare questions about priorities and success measures.','For a leadership question, describe the situation, your decision, what changed, and what you learned.','Do not memorize a life story; prepare adaptable evidence.','Write three short stories that show a relevant skill, decision, and result.'],
  'how-to-answer-tell-me-about-yourself': ['How to Answer Tell Me About Yourself','professional development','heard','This question is an invitation to offer a relevant narrative, not every detail of your background.','Use present role, relevant path, and reason for this opportunity. Keep the connection to the role explicit.','“I lead customer research now; before that I worked in support, which is why I care about turning feedback into product decisions.”','Do not start at childhood or list every job.','Draft a 45-second answer with present, past, and next.'],
  'how-to-network-at-an-event': ['How to Network at an Event','conversation skills','heard','Networking is more useful when the goal is a real exchange instead of collecting the most contacts.','Choose a few conversations, ask about current work, share a relevant detail, and follow up only where there is a genuine reason.','“What kind of problem is your team spending the most time on?” opens a substantive conversation.','Do not treat people as leads to process.','Set one learning goal for your next event and prepare two questions.'],
  'how-to-prepare-for-a-negotiation': ['How to Prepare for a Negotiation','professional conversations','heard','Negotiation improves when you know your interests, limits, alternatives, and the other side’s likely constraints.','Define your preferred outcome, acceptable range, walk-away point, and questions that reveal what the other party values.','Before discussing price, ask what timing, support, or terms matter most to the other side.','Do not negotiate from a vague sense of what you want.','Write your target, minimum, alternative, and three questions before a negotiation.'],
  'how-to-make-a-case-for-more-resources': ['How to Make a Case for More Resources','persuasion','heard','A resource request is stronger when it connects investment to an outcome leaders already value.','Describe the current constraint, evidence of its cost, the resource requested, and what would change if approved.','“One additional reviewer would reduce the release backlog by two weeks and prevent missed compliance checks.”','Do not make the case solely from how hard the team is working.','Draft a request using constraint, impact, resource, and expected result.'],
  'how-to-run-a-retrospective': ['How to Run a Useful Retrospective','reflection','learning','A retrospective helps a team learn when it moves from events to one or two practical changes.','Review what happened, what helped, what hindered, and which experiment to run next. Assign an owner for the experiment.','After a late release, identify whether unclear ownership—not effort—caused the delay, then test an ownership checklist.','Do not turn the meeting into a complaint archive.','Run a 20-minute review and end with one owned experiment.'],
  'how-to-learn-a-tool-quickly': ['How to Learn a New Tool Quickly','skill acquisition','learning','Learning a tool is faster when you begin with a real task instead of trying to absorb every feature.','Choose one outcome, follow a short guided attempt, and save a reference for the errors you encounter.','To learn a spreadsheet feature, build the report you need rather than browsing menus.','Do not confuse watching tutorials with performing the task.','Pick one task your new tool must help you complete this week.'],
  'how-to-avoid-tutorial-overload': ['How to Avoid Tutorial Overload','learning methods','learning','Tutorial overload happens when consuming explanations replaces making attempts.','Set a limit on input, then switch to a small output that forces you to use what you just learned.','Watch ten minutes of a coding lesson, then build the tiny feature before watching more.','Do not collect courses as proof of progress.','For your next tutorial, decide the five-minute practice task before pressing play.'],
  'how-to-learn-a-skill-with-a-busy-schedule': ['How to Learn a Skill with a Busy Schedule','learning systems','learning','Busy schedules reward practice designs that are small, prepared, and connected to an existing cue.','Use short protected blocks, prepare materials beforehand, and choose a minimum version for disrupted days.','A ten-minute vocabulary recall drill after lunch can be more durable than a weekly heroic session.','Do not wait for an empty week to start.','Schedule two short sessions and write the exact first action for each.'],
  'how-to-return-to-a-skill-after-a-break': ['How to Return to a Skill After a Break','practice systems','learning','Returning after a break works best when you restart with evidence rather than trying to prove you have not lost progress.','Do a gentle baseline attempt, identify what remains solid, and choose an easier first week of practice.','After a month away from piano, play a familiar piece slowly and mark only the passages that need rebuilding.','Do not punish yourself with an excessive catch-up session.','Make one low-pressure baseline attempt before planning your return.'],
  'how-to-build-a-portfolio-of-work': ['How to Build a Portfolio of Work','professional development','learning','A portfolio makes your capabilities easier to evaluate by showing work, context, and decisions.','Select a few representative pieces and explain the problem, your role, the process, and the result without exaggeration.','A case study can show how you clarified a confusing customer journey and what you changed after testing.','Do not include work that you cannot explain or share responsibly.','Choose one project and write four headings: problem, role, approach, result.'],
  'how-to-reflect-on-a-project': ['How to Reflect on a Project','reflection','learning','Project reflection preserves lessons that would otherwise be lost once the next deadline arrives.','Review the original goal, outcome, surprises, decisions, and one change you would make next time.','If a launch succeeded but exhausted the team, capture the process cost alongside the business result.','Do not write only about what went wrong.','Schedule 15 minutes after a project and answer five reflection questions in writing.']
});

for (const [slug, detail] of Object.entries(guideDetails)) {
  guides.push([slug, detail[0], detail[1], detail[2], detail[3], detail[4]]);
}

